{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>His sons' daughters</b>: Serach bat Asher and Yocheved bat Levi — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 46:7), and what does it matter that he multiplies [the word] in \"his daughters\"? Rather, the practice of Scripture, when it speaks of genealogies of the many, is to use a plural form for a single individual: \"וּבְנֵי דָן חוּשִׁים\" (Genesis 46:23), \"וּבְנֵי פַלּוּא אֱלִיאָב\" (Numbers 26:8). And likewise \"his daughters\" — Dinah, \"and his sons' daughters\" — Serach bat Asher; but Yocheved, Scripture does not mention her, as it says, \"all the souls, sixty-six.\" Rather, it alludes to her, according to the opinion of our Sages (Bava Batra 123a)."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 46, :notes {0 "The phrase 'ומה ירבה בבנותיו' is Ramban's objection to Rashi: Scripture already said 'his daughters,' so why add 'his sons' daughters' to arrive at only two individuals? The resolution follows: Scripture uses plural forms even for single individuals in genealogical contexts."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:45:24.241908624Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}